1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:13,651
On the evening of the eleventh of July, 2015, a prisoner in Mexico's most secure prison walked to the shower area of his cell, crouched behind a low wall, and vanished.

2
00:00:14,751 --> 00:00:23,583
Beneath the shower was a tunnel about one and a half kilometres long, with lighting, ventilation, and a motorbike running on rails.

3
00:00:24,683 --> 00:00:29,184
It was the second time he had escaped from a maximum-security prison.

4
00:00:30,284 --> 00:00:34,977
Four years later, he was serving a life sentence in the United States.

5
00:00:36,077 --> 00:00:53,400
Joaquín Guzmán, known as El Chapo, became the most famous drug lord since Pablo Escobar. In 2019 a jury in New York convicted him of running one of the largest drug-trafficking organisations in the world. This is how he built it.

6
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:08,656
Joaquín Archivaldo Guzmán Loera was born, by most accounts in 1957, in La Tuna, a hamlet in the mountains of Sinaloa, in north-western Mexico.

7
00:01:09,756 --> 00:01:18,289
His family was poor. As a boy he worked in the fields, and in these mountains, opium poppies and marijuana were among the crops.

8
00:01:19,389 --> 00:01:27,240
He left school early. Because he was short, about one metre sixty-eight, he was nicknamed El Chapo, which means Shorty.

9
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:39,220
As a young man he went to work for Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo, the most powerful trafficker in Mexico, whose network became known as the Guadalajara cartel.

10
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:56,097
In the 1980s, Colombian cartels began moving cocaine through Mexico to reach the United States. Mexican traffickers carried it across the border, and soon took a share of the drugs as payment.

11
00:01:57,197 --> 00:02:09,592
When Félix Gallardo was arrested in 1989, his network split into rival groups. Guzmán and his partners took control of the routes run from Sinaloa.

12
00:02:10,692 --> 00:02:18,991
His most important partner was Ismael Zambada, known as El Mayo, a cautious trafficker who would stay free for decades.

13
00:02:20,091 --> 00:02:35,771
He became known for innovation. In 1990, American agents found a tunnel running under the border, from a house in Agua Prieta in Mexico to a warehouse in Arizona. It had been built for his organisation.

14
00:02:36,871 --> 00:02:57,671
His feud with the Arellano Félix brothers of Tijuana turned deadly. In May 1993, gunmen opened fire at Guadalajara airport, killing a Catholic cardinal, Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo, and six others. Investigators concluded the cardinal had been caught up in an attack aimed at Guzmán.

15
00:02:58,771 --> 00:03:07,027
Weeks later, Guzmán was arrested in Guatemala and handed over to Mexico. He was sentenced to about twenty years.

16
00:03:09,227 --> 00:03:21,045
In prison, he paid off guards and lived in comfort. On the nineteenth of January, 2001, he escaped, reportedly hidden in a laundry cart, with help from prison staff.

17
00:03:22,145 --> 00:03:32,193
For the next thirteen years he lived on the run, mostly in the mountains of Sinaloa, moving between hideouts and protected by bribes and loyal gunmen.

18
00:03:33,293 --> 00:03:46,584
In those years, the Sinaloa cartel grew into one of the largest drug-trafficking organisations in the world, moving cocaine, heroin, marijuana and methamphetamine into the United States.

19
00:03:47,684 --> 00:03:57,433
In 2009, Forbes magazine estimated his fortune at about a billion dollars, and put him on its list of the world's billionaires.

20
00:03:58,533 --> 00:04:08,944
In parts of Sinaloa he was treated as a hero. Ballads known as narcocorridos celebrated him, and the cartel handed out money and favours.

21
00:04:11,144 --> 00:04:20,872
In December 2006, President Felipe Calderón sent the army against the cartels. The cartels fought the state, and each other.

22
00:04:21,972 --> 00:04:32,937
In 2008 the cartel itself split. Guzmán's former allies, the Beltrán Leyva brothers, turned against him, and the killing spread through Sinaloa.

23
00:04:34,037 --> 00:04:48,011
Guzmán's cartel fought its rivals for control of the border crossings. In Ciudad Juárez, where Sinaloa fought the Juárez cartel, about three thousand people were murdered in 2010 alone.

24
00:04:49,111 --> 00:05:02,209
His organisation bribed police, soldiers and officials. It moved drugs by tunnel, plane, truck and even home-made submarine, and used hitmen against rivals and suspected informers.

25
00:05:03,309 --> 00:05:13,720
Across Mexico, tens of thousands of people were killed in drug-related violence during Calderón's six years in office, and thousands more disappeared.

26
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:28,144
On the twenty-second of February, 2014, Mexican marines, working with American agents, caught him in a beachfront apartment in Mazatlán, without a shot being fired.

27
00:05:29,244 --> 00:05:38,247
He was held at Altiplano, the country's most secure prison. Seventeen months later, he disappeared through the tunnel under his shower.

28
00:05:39,347 --> 00:05:48,093
The escape humiliated the Mexican government. Investigators said it could not have happened without help from inside the prison.

29
00:05:49,193 --> 00:06:01,567
In January 2016, marines tracked him to a house in Los Mochis. After a gunfight, he fled through the city's drains, and was caught on a highway in a stolen car.

30
00:06:02,667 --> 00:06:16,235
Officials said his contacts with actors and producers, for a film about his life, had helped them find him. The day after his capture, a magazine published an interview he had given the American actor Sean Penn.

31
00:06:18,435 --> 00:06:25,859
On the nineteenth of January, 2017, Mexico extradited him to the United States.

32
00:06:26,959 --> 00:06:36,559
His trial in Brooklyn lasted about three months. Former associates testified against him, describing bribes, drug shipments and killings.

33
00:06:37,659 --> 00:06:54,832
Some witnesses claimed that bribes had reached the highest levels of Mexican politics. Most of the officials named denied it. One, the former security minister Genaro García Luna, was later convicted in a US court of taking cartel bribes.

34
00:06:55,932 --> 00:07:06,215
On the twelfth of February, 2019, the jury found him guilty on all ten counts, including running a continuing criminal enterprise.

35
00:07:07,315 --> 00:07:17,000
That July, he was sentenced to life in prison plus thirty years, and ordered to forfeit twelve point six billion dollars. His appeals have failed.

36
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:31,967
In 2021, his wife, Emma Coronel Aispuro, was sentenced to three years in a US prison after admitting she had helped his drug business and his 2015 escape.

37
00:07:34,167 --> 00:07:44,407
His cartel did not collapse. His sons, known as Los Chapitos, and his long-time partner Ismael Zambada, known as El Mayo, kept it running.

38
00:07:45,507 --> 00:08:03,448
In July 2024, Zambada and one of Guzmán's sons were arrested in Texas after landing in a small plane. Zambada said he had been kidnapped. Their arrest set off a war between cartel factions in Sinaloa that has killed hundreds of people.

39
00:08:04,548 --> 00:08:21,039
US authorities say the Sinaloa cartel is now one of the main suppliers of fentanyl, the drug behind most American overdose deaths. In 2025, the United States designated it a terrorist organisation.

40
00:08:22,139 --> 00:08:30,224
Mexico's official register lists more than a hundred thousand missing people, most of them since the drug war began.

41
00:08:31,324 --> 00:08:41,607
For all the songs and television series about him, his story is mostly lived by others: the murdered, the disappeared, and the families still searching for them.

42
00:08:42,707 --> 00:08:52,392
El Chapo was caught three times, and is now locked away for life. His trade is not. Catching the man at the top, it turned out, was the easy part.
